{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2953","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2953","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2953\",\"parent_revision\":\"384c9f559bede4c8fa93d6aab2159ff101dad75e\",\"root_revision\":\"a7b9f7bc26a220d6804282d4193094e3aae803ca8b5a2a708a8ba70207ddf8b4\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Refactor\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-17\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`github_contents.py` keeps 5 custom Protocol shims so test doubles can structurally satisfy PyGithub types, because PyGithub's real classes have private, requester-bound constructors that a test double cannot nominally satisfy (see PR #2939). PyGithub is fully typed (`py.typed`, confirmed), so the Protocols are not a missing-types workaround \u2014 they exist only because tests cannot construct real PyGithub objects directly.\\n\\nPyGithub's own test suite avoids this by mocking at the HTTP layer instead of the class layer: a `Framework.TestCase` + `ReplayData` system that intercepts requests and replays recorded responses, so tests exercise genuine `Repository`/`ContentFile` objects built from real API responses rather than hand-rolled doubles. PyGithub 2.9.0 (pinned in this repo) depends on `requests` for transport, so an HTTP-layer recorder such as `vcrpy` or `responses` could in principle replace the 5 Protocol shims with real PyGithub objects constructed from a one-time recorded cassette against the real GitHub API.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: This is a proposed testing-architecture improvement to investigate and prototype, not a confirmed defect. Feasibility is unverified \u2014 specifically whether `github_contents.py`'s test setup routes all PyGithub calls through `requests` in a way `vcrpy` can intercept cleanly.\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-17T23:48:05.121856Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: refactor: backlog_core's github_contents.py test doubles use custom Protocol shims instead of PyGithub's own HTTP-replay testing pattern\\nDate: 2026-08-18\\nGoal: Determine whether and how to replace github_contents.py's Protocol-shim test doubles with HTTP-layer replay testing so tests exercise genuine PyGithub objects.\\nConditions:\\n1. Does github_contents.py's test setup route PyGithub calls through requests in a way vcrpy/responses could intercept? | Status: AVAILABLE\\n2. Are vcrpy/responses already dependencies, and would adding one conflict with this repo's dependency conventions (no uv workspace, PEP 723)? | Status: AVAILABLE\\n3. Does PyGithub publish its own reusable ReplayData/Framework.TestCase test utilities? | Status: AVAILABLE\\n4. How many Protocol shims actually exist in github_contents.py (item states 5; investigation found 7)? | Status: AVAILABLE\\n5. Which of the three viable implementation paths (live-recorded cassettes, responses-based matchers, or hybrid) best fits this repo's test-suite performance and CI-credential constraints? | Status: DERIVABLE\\nAVAILABLE count: 4\\nDERIVABLE count: 1\\nMISSING count: 0\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 discovered while verifying PR #2939's Protocol-shim rationale\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-17\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the codebase**, I want to **backlog_core's github_contents.py test doubles use custom protocol shims instead of pygithub's own http-replay testing pattern** so that **the code is cleaner and more maintainable**.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"b7ea2ff57407553e525b70cdddca3a052c947f64973e7f8bf22ce8beb472a3fd\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPTEgSg\"}"}